=== THE CLAIM UNDER TEST ===
  STRESS-PILLAR-DECISION.md reports T1 0.5372 -> 0.6743 (+0.137) for the stress slot.
  measured here: 0.5372 -> 0.6743 (+0.1371) on 4 onsets
     CSRS 2-pillar: AUC 0.5372  p_shift 0.4265  p_aaft 0.3698  no-signal 95th pct 0.7432  -> NOT SIGNIFICANT
     CSRS 3-pillar: AUC 0.6743  p_shift 0.1201  p_aaft 0.0533  no-signal 95th pct 0.7078  -> NOT SIGNIFICANT

  A per-configuration AUC can only be called an improvement if
  the higher value clears its own null. Reporting a difference
  between two numbers that both sit inside their nulls is not a
  detection result.

=== MULTIPLICITY (Holm, on max(p_shift, p_aaft)) ===
  15 series screened. Holm: reject p_(i) only while p_(i) <= 0.05/(15-i+1).
     1.         dd_accel  p=0.0007  threshold=0.00333  survives
     2.         rv_accel  p=0.0020  threshold=0.00357  survives
     3.      jump_int_20  p=0.0053  threshold=0.00385  does NOT survive
  survives Holm across all 15 comparisons: ['dd_accel', 'rv_accel']
  A nominal p just under 0.05 from a 15-way screen is not a finding.
  Measured type-I error of this test is ~8% at nominal 5%
  (docs/evidence/EARLY-WARNING-POWER.md), so treat 0.04-0.05 as
  borderline rather than as a pass.

  Onset counts differ by span, so AUCs across rows are NOT
  comparable (ARCHITECTURE D5(b)). Each row is judged only against
  its own null.
  T1 is partly TAUTOLOGICAL for volatility-derived signals: FR32
  crisis windows are high-volatility windows by construction (mean
  rv_t_yz 0.6109 inside vs 0.4979 outside). A T1 pass is therefore
  not independent evidence of systemic-risk detection, and it says
  nothing at all about advance warning.
